    {
      "id": "method-node-design-instructions",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Method Contract Sketch",
      "root": false,
      "position": {
        "x": -2060,
        "y": 260
      },
      "width": 760,
      "height": 300,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 210
        },
        {
          "id": "guided-by",
          "key": "guided-by",
          "label": "guided by",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 180,
          "role": "relationship",
          "allowedEdgeTypes": [
            "reference"
          ],
          "metadata": {
            "relationship": true,
            "role": "relationship"
          }
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "method-node-design"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# Method contract (sketch)\n\nA Method is the reusable half of \"when this happens, do that.\" The Event Handler chooses the Event and connects the Methods; the Method owns the logic, what it needs, and what it may change. Field names are illustrative.\n\n## Method declares\n\n- **Inputs.** Named inputs read from the occurrence payload, for example `value` and `previousValue`. Required by default. A missing required input fails the Invocation before any code runs, naming the Method and the input.\n- **Effects.** A short list of kinds: update node, create node, delete node, edges, emit event, hand off, with optional targets. No target means any node in scope, and the Handler's summary says so. `allowMutations` is derived from it, not authored.\n\n## Invocation (runtime, not persisted)\n\n```text\nInvocation {\n  id\n  methodId\n  caller        Handler id, Method index and count\n  arguments\n  context       fragment scope\n  mode          dry or live\n  cause         occurrenceId, rootOccurrenceId, deliveryId\n}\n\nOutcome {\n  invocationId\n  status\n  result | error\n  attemptedEffects\n  duration\n}\n```\n\n`api.invocation` is the frozen Invocation as the source sees it. A dry run returns the proposed effects instead of applying them. The Method card shows its last Outcome and which Handler and Event caused it.\n\n## Authority\n\n```text\nMethod declares what it may do\n   and the caller has authority to do it\n   and the runtime permits it here\n=> the call runs\n```\n\nThe host is where this is enforced today, and where a Personal Server would add effects such as reading files or sending mail.\n\n## The Event Handler\n\n- Shows a read-only summary of its Methods' declared effects, derived from the Methods.\n- Runs its Methods in edge order. Continue-after-failure is the Handler's default failure policy, not part of what a Method is. Stop, retry, roll back, branch or a failure handler are later Handler policies.\n\n## Not in scope yet\n\nAsynchronous or long-running work, progress, cancellation, retries, and persisted Invocation nodes or history. Invocation has an identity so these can be added without retrofitting causality."
        },
        "interfaceContract": {
          "version": 1,
          "receivesViewContent": true
        }
      },
