      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": false,
      "data": {
        "title": "A Method Is a Reviewable Contract",
        "statement": "A Method should be a small, reviewable contract: the inputs it reads, the effects it is allowed to have, and the outcome it reports. Each call is an Invocation, an explicit runtime entity with its own identity that is not persisted in the graph, so its outcome and effects can be correlated with the Event occurrence that caused it. That is enough for an Event Handler to call a Method safely, for a reviewer to see what it will do without reading its source, and for a dry run to show what would change.",
        "status": "framed",
        "confidence": "unproven",
        "notes": "Deliberately narrow: it covers a Method that an Event Handler invokes once per occurrence and that finishes, not asynchronous or long-running work. Invocation stays lightweight and ephemeral for now, but it has an identity from the start so lifecycle, progress, cancellation, persistence and retries can be added later without retrofitting causality. What happens after a Method fails is the Handler's composition policy, not part of what a Method is. It builds on What an Event Handler Node Should Do (github://mikemartinez1974/public/ideas/event-handler-behavior/root.node); the wider Methods topic is github://mikemartinez1974/public/topics/twilite-scripting-system/methods/root.node.",
        "definitionKey": "idea",
